.TH CJSON_SERIALIZE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_serialize \- serialize a DOM tree to a compact JSON string
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

char *cjson_serialize(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Walks the DOM tree under node and writes it as a compact JSON string
with no whitespace.  A NULL node gives the string "null".

Object members come out in insertion order (see
cjson_dictionary_first(3)) and array elements in index order, so one
tree gives the same output on every call and in every process.  A
document that is parsed and serialized again keeps the order of its
members.

A double is written in the shortest decimal form that reads back as
the same value, which has at most 17 significant digits.  The
decimal separator is always '.', as RFC 8259 asks, whatever the
LC_NUMERIC locale of the calling thread.  An integer is written with
%lld, and a double that is not finite (Inf and NaN) as "null".

The output is always valid JSON text.  Every string in a tree, value
or object key, is well-formed UTF-8, because the library checks that
wherever bytes enter a tree (see cjson_parse(3) and
cjson_create_string(3)).  So the serializer writes string content
unchanged and adds only the escapes that JSON itself needs.

A subtree that nests more than 500 levels deep is refused and the
function returns NULL for it.  Each array or object counts as one
level, in any combination, and a scalar adds none.  The parser has
the same limit, and only the construction API (cjson_list_push() and
cjson_dictionary_set()) can build a deeper tree, since it has no
depth bound of its own.  The limit is a policy and has nothing to do
with the stack size of the calling thread: the serializer walks the
nesting with an explicit worklist on the heap, so a tree at the
limit uses the same small, fixed amount of native stack as a flat
one.  An ordinary tree never comes near this depth.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, a null-terminated string on the heap.
NULL when an allocation fails or when node nests more than 500
levels deep.
The string comes from the root node's allocator.  When the default
allocator built the tree, the caller frees it with
cjson_serialize_free(), and when a custom allocator built it, with
cjson_serialize_free_mp() and that same allocator.  Calling
cjson_serialize_free() on a buffer from a custom allocator hands it
to plain free(), which never allocated it.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_serialize_pretty (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free_mp (3),
.BR cjson_create_string (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cjson_parse (3)
